        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The objective of this test was to assess nutrient intake and status during bed rest and to assure correction of nutritional status following bed rest.  <div>
<div>
APPROACH:<br>
Assessment of body composition was required to determine changes in muscle and bone compartments.  The nutritional assessment measured dietary intake and determined nutritional status through blood samples and 48-hour void-by-void urine samples.  
</div><div>
Nutritional assessment is required to determine adequacy of nutrient stores prior to bed rest, to assess nutrient intake and status during bed rest, and to assure correction of nutritional status following bed rest. Assessment of body composition is required to determine changes in muscle and bone compartments during space flight.
</div><div>
Nutritional status was expected to decline/remain unchanged during bed rest. A decrease in bone mineral density and an increase in markers of bone resorption were expected.
</div><div>
The biological samples collected were used for determination of nutritional status, which included the following: </div><ul><li>Protein status
</li><li>Calcium and bone status
</li><li>Mineral status
</li><li>Antioxidant status
</li><li>General blood chemistry
</li><li>Water-soluble vitamin status
</li><li>Fat-soluble vitamin status
</li><li>Iron status</li><li>Renal stone risk</li><li>General urine chemistry</li></ul><div><br></div><div>RESULTS:<br>
This experiment has concluded. Tabular results are available for the public. Refer to cited publications for more information.</div></div>
        </div>

